    "q-161745": {
      "dom_id": "step-q161745",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "$$\n{∭}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{∗}{\\int }_{∗}^{∗}{\\int }_{∗}^{b}f(x,y,z)\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx\n$$\n\nThe triple integral above is defined over the type III region given by\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,-2\\le z\\le {x}^{2}+2,\\,-2-z\\le y\\le 2+x+z\\}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $a-b?$",
      "worked_solution": "A type III region in three-dimensional space can be written as\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,{u}_{1}(x,z)\\le y\\le {u}_{2}(x,z)\\}.\n$$\n\nA typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14217845/assets/0603bed12c6af74e46544d8bebc52362aed95a9a5ad7418ea4faaf228f3b5241.png)\n\n \n\nIf $R$ is a type III region, we can express the triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over $R$ as a mixed integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y]\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x,z)=-2-z,\\,{u}_{2}(x,z)=2+x+z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our triple integral can be written as\n\n$$\n{∭}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{-2-z}^{2+x+z}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dA.\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,-2\\le z\\le {x}^{2}+2\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by expressing the double integral over $D$ as a repeated integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{-2-z}^{2+x+z}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-2}^{{x}^{2}+2}[{\\int }_{-2-z}^{2+x+z}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dz\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-2}^{{x}^{2}+2}{\\int }_{-2-z}^{2+x+z}f(x,y,z)\\,dy\\,dz\\,dx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na-b=(0)-(2+x+z)=-2-x-z.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
