    "q-161725": {
      "dom_id": "step-q161725",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "$$\n{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{x}^{\\sqrt{1-z}}4xy\\,dy]dA,\n$$\n\nEvaluate the repeated integral above given that $D$ is a region in the $xz$-plane bounded by the lines $z=0,$ $x=0,$ and the curve $z+{x}^{2}=1.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y,$ treating $x$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{x}^{\\sqrt{1-z}}4xy\\,dy]dA & ={∬}_{D}2x[{y}^{2}]_{y=x}^{y=\\sqrt{1-z}}\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x(1-z-{x}^{2})\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\,2x(1-z)-2{x}^{3}\\,)\\,dA\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to calculate this double integral, we plot $D$ as a type I plane region in the $xz$-plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14217845/assets/8079d8f9796c1f863a515ced0091c1f420318be1c4bfe0eb2da641bddfa426ce.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, to evaluate our double integral as a repeated integral over this region, we integrate with respect to $z$ and then with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(\\,2x(1-z)-2{x}^{3}\\,)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-{x}^{2}}(\\,2x(1-z)-2{x}^{3}\\,)\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(x[-(1-z)^{2}]_{z=0}^{z=1-{x}^{2}}-2{x}^{3}[z]_{z=0}^{z=1-{x}^{2}}\\,)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-{x}^{5}+x-2{x}^{3}+2{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{5}+x-2{x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{6}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{4}]}_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
