    "q-161764": {
      "dom_id": "step-q161764",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "Evaluate the triple integral \n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}4xy\\,dV\n$$\n\nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,x\\le y\\le \\sqrt{1-z}\\},\n$$\n\nand $D$ is a finite region in $xz$-plane enclosed between the $x$-axis, the $z$-axis, and the curve $z=1-{x}^{2},$ in the first quadrant.",
      "worked_solution": "Writing down our triple integral as a mixed integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}4xy\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{x}^{\\sqrt{1-z}}4xy\\,dy]dA.\n$$\n\nFirst, we evaluate the inner integral with respect to $y,$ treating $x$ and $z$ as constants:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{x}^{\\sqrt{1-z}}4xy\\,dy]dA & ={∬}_{D}2x[{y}^{2}]_{y=x}^{y=\\sqrt{1-z}}\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x(\\,(1-z)-{x}^{2})\\,dA \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x(1-z)-2{x}^{3}\\,dA\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe must now evaluate this double integral over the region $D$ in the $xz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14217845/assets/95a2b4e28dbfae5ce447b3b975b2f74bf04883441217f42a24636970948402a0.png)\n\n  \n\nWe can evaluate our double integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2x(1-z)-2{x}^{3}\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-z}}2x(1-z)-2{x}^{3}\\,dx\\,dz \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-z)[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-z}}2x\\,dx]dz+{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-z}}-2{x}^{3}\\,dx]dz \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-z)[{x}^{2}]_{x=0}^{x=\\sqrt{1-z}}dz+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}]}_{x=0}^{x=\\sqrt{1-z}}dz \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-z)^{2}\\,dz-\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-z)^{2}\\,dz \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-z)^{2}\\,dz \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{[-(1-z)^{3}]}_{z=0}^{z=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
